WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.030
Whale strikes did happen,
and there were angry whales

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.520
that would pursue boats of whalers

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.830
when they were out hunting
them to take them down.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.390
Hi. I'm Jim Delgado.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.270
I'm a maritime archeologist and historian.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.550
I've been in the business for 45 years

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.640
and worked on over 100
shipwreck investigations.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.250
Today we're going to be
looking at shipwrecks

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.817
in movies and TV and
judge how real they are.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:34.714
Lost. Not gone.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:37.920
Didn't happen.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.270
It's pure Hollywood.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.920
We're talking about Magellan's ships.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.620
Fernão de Magalhães,
or Ferdinand Magellan,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.580
as he was called,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.060
went out into the Pacific
in the early 16th century.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.018
He starts out with five ships,

00:00:50.018 --> 00:00:52.684
and basically one gets home.

00:00:52.684 --> 00:00:54.900
The wrecks that were left behind,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.760
one went up on a beach and was burned,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.200
the other one sank.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.993
So discovering a ship from Magellan

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:03.243
in an underground cave in the Philippines,

00:01:03.243 --> 00:01:04.426
ain't gonna happen.

00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:12.990
Cloves.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.580
Those cloves would have been,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.353
first off, not even still in a barrel.

00:01:16.353 --> 00:01:18.330
They would not have retained any smell.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.087
But there's more to it
that also just makes me go,

00:01:22.087 --> 00:01:23.730
"Oh, yeah. Right."

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.710
They're sitting in a cave. It's open.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.088
Rain's going to get in there,

00:01:27.088 --> 00:01:30.480
and dry rot is going to set in real fast.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.490
The fact that you still have sails intact

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.440
after 400 years, no.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.823
That you have the wood intact,
that you can walk around, no.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.753
Here's your gold you've been looking for.

00:01:42.261 --> 00:01:43.320
You happy now?

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.400
Oh, no.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.900
I've only worked on one wreck

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.050
where we found a tiny little
bag of 58 silver coins.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.090
This myth of shipwrecks having treasure

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.850
has been disastrous for archeology.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.730
We know more about ancient
Roman wrecks, for example,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.100
than we do about the ships
from this time period

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.710
because everybody thinks
they're full of treasure

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.710
and treasure hunters have just
torn them apart in the past.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.245
That drives me crazy.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.100
Holy s---.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.990
The gold? Yeah. It would have survived.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.160
It would've been that way,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.290
but you start kicking gold over like that,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.990
that beautiful mask would've been dented.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.710
Gold is soft.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.857
So unless he wanted to melt it down,

00:02:21.857 --> 00:02:24.849
he was really not being
a smart treasure hunter.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.060
That chopper would not have been able

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.010
to pick this big, heavy thing up.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.470
It's not only got the
whole entire wooden hull,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.920
it's got ballast. It's
full of rock at the bottom.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.090
The condition this thing is in,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.670
no matter the net or not,
it would've broken open.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:48.240
Whenever we raise a ship,
we do so piece by piece.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.230
For about every dollar we
spend in excavating a ship,

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.890
we spend over $10 to take
care of it and to preserve it.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.250
So it's rare when we'll
actually raise an entire ship.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.759
I'd give this a one out of
10 just for the eye candy.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.233
No way.

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.790
I'd never try to thread
a needle like this.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.080
It's way too tight,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.820
and all the wrecked ships

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.680
would be a clear warning sign to me

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.140
to not go there unless I
had a really good reason.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.100
What we generally call a setting
like this is a "ship trap,"

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.530
dangerous places where
you've got a narrow passage.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.790
They're places with reefs

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.507
that generally aren't seen
until the last minute,

00:03:32.507 --> 00:03:35.490
and that includes spots
like Bermuda and its reefs,

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.050
where there's plenty of wrecks,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.290
or the Dry Tortugas,
famous in pirate lore,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.777
off the coast of Florida.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.100
Terrible as that is,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.050
you see sharks occasionally around wrecks

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.540
because wrecks usually attract fish.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.000
For sharks, that's
ringing the dinner bell.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.916
I'll give it a three out of 10.

00:03:59.228 --> 00:04:02.370
OK, take her up and over the bow rail.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.170
The submersibles are real.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:05.490
They're the Mir-1 and the Mir-2.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.340
They're no longer working,
but I've dived in those subs.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.570
I was part of an expedition in 2000,

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.430
and I came back as chief scientist.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.400
We completely mapped all
of Titanic on the outside.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.110
These subs are 6 feet in diameter.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.030
Nickel steel, really thick.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.800
Those tiny little portholes are set in,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.270
made of thick Lucite,

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.560
because you have to withstand

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.200
6,000-pounds-per-square-inch pressure.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.740
This gives you a sense of
just what the pressure is.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.020
This is an ordinary Styrofoam cup

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.873
that I brought down in a mesh bag

00:04:35.873 --> 00:04:37.470
tied to the outside of the sub.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.190
This full-scale Styrofoam
cup was made this small

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.258
and this tough by
6,000-pounds-per-square-inch pressure.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.650
That's real footage.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.350
So, in making the movie,

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.500
Cameron's actually explored
a great deal of Titanic,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.790
and those things that dangle down,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.650
we see them on deep-water metal wrecks.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.120
They're called rusticles,

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.390
but it's not really a
process of corrosion.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.430
There's a bacteria that lives down there

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.700
called titanicae Halomonas that
was discovered on the wreck,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.770
and it actually eats the metal.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.170
Titanic is slowly being eaten,

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.630
but it's not going to
go away anytime soon.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.335
The scientists that we've worked with

00:05:23.335 --> 00:05:25.440
estimate that there will still be

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.524
a recognizable hull of
Titanic for hundreds of years.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.140
OK, let's drop down to B deck.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.400
OK, A deck.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.220
This is absolutely 100% accurate.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.270
These robots are ROVs, for
remotely operated vehicles.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.120
In my last mission to Titanic, in 2010,

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.730
we didn't go in subs, we sent those down,

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:53.490
and with these robots and
with lighting systems,

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.015
you could see so much more of Titanic.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:05.959
Brock: That's Hockley's bed.

00:06:05.959 --> 00:06:06.867
[James sighs]

00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:08.430
This brings back a lot of memories.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.550
Everything we saw, for
the most part, is real.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.440
You see personal items.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.920
I've never seen the piano,
but it makes perfect sense.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.500
Having seen other footage that
didn't make it into the movie

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:20.970
from Jim's dives,

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:24.540
where you see remains of chests

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.820
that still have chinaware in them.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.110
When they went into that cabin,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.150
the first thing you
saw was that fireplace.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:31.920
That's real. The bed is real.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.620
Now, then they reproduced that in the film

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.660
to give you that sense of being inside.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.750
So I can see the difference
in what was shot on the wreck

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.730
with what was shot on set.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.340
Those are the crisper,
clearer, more color in them.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.790
Whereas on Titanic, the
stuff's a little bluer,

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:52.260
and thanks to that work
that Jim and the team did,

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.900
we have a much better sense
of the interior of Titanic

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.790
that we never would've had before.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:59.623
I'd give this film a 10 out of 10.

00:07:02.572 --> 00:07:03.405
Hit.

00:07:05.173 --> 00:07:06.118
Hit.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.621
Yes. Oof.

00:07:10.621 --> 00:07:13.680
There's always a bit
of license in a movie.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.010
But I think this is a
pretty good impression

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:20.015
of what happened when the USS Indianapolis

00:07:20.015 --> 00:07:23.220
was torpedoed by the
Japanese submarine I-58

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.810
towards the end of World War II.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:28.980
Of close to 1,200 guys, 316
were ultimately rescued.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.650
This is the single greatest loss of life

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:34.650
in a single ship that
the US Navy suffered.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.890
It looked like three torpedoes
were launched from the I-58.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.500
We have a sense from survivor accounts

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.350
and looking at the wreckage
that at least two hit,

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:48.210
and one did hit near the bow
and basically blew it off.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.827
The other one hit amidships,
which we also see happening.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:57.330
Hitting it amidships, right in the heart,

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:00.060
not only opened up another big hole,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.470
it also killed the power.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.230
And you see that the engine
room is not responding.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.455
They have lost steam.
They've got fuel oil on fire.

00:08:07.455 --> 00:08:10.140
Electrical systems are down.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.515
That was the fatal blow.

00:08:13.866 --> 00:08:15.950
Jump now!

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:18.420
As to whether or not the torpedo's
going all the way through

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.240
or that's other things, it's hard to say.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.670
Now, I've looked at a
merchant ship torpedoed

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.280
by a Japanese ship using
the same type of torpedoes.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.430
What I saw was it creates a bubble

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:30.960
of super-pressurized water

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.293
that just tears through the
steel like it's wet cardboard.

00:08:37.922 --> 00:08:39.021
Jump!

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:43.500
You couldn't stay on the ship.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.120
It was going to be sure death.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.070
If you can't get that fire out,

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.620
the smartest thing to
do is to abandon ship.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.510
Generally, abandon ship is supposed to be

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.108
a more orderly thing, but
in something like this,

00:08:54.108 --> 00:08:56.190
you have flames everywhere,

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.290
and men were walking through those fires,

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.493
they were being burned,
and it's full of fuel oil

00:09:00.493 --> 00:09:02.280
that is rapidly spreading the fire

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:04.770
not only throughout the
ship but on the water.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.290
Clearly, in that case, there's
no time to get everybody

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.450
in the boats and do this
in an orderly fashion.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.750
So, pretty accurate,
particularly in wartime.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.250
The way they depict the sinking

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.340
more or less confirms not
only what the survivors said

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.460
but also Capt. Hashimoto of
the I-58, who, with his crew,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.980
witnessed the end of Indianapolis.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.720
But what also helps inform it

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.450
was the rediscovery of the
wreck deep in the Pacific.

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:36.060
And that is how a big ship like that,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.710
even a heavily armored warship,

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:40.530
no power, no pumps, fires everywhere,

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:44.100
the ship open to the sea is
going to go down that fast.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.788
I would give this a nine out of 10.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:54.669
I can't watch this without thinking,

00:09:54.669 --> 00:09:57.960
"There but for the grace of God go I."

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:02.520
This story speaks to a ship
that only became famous

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.470
because an amazing book
was written about it

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:05.610
and then a film.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:09.180
But in 1991, Andrea
Gail, a fishing vessel,

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.630
sails out in this perfect storm

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:14.940
that claimed them and other ships.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:16.980
Many years ago, I was part
of a team that went out

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:21.240
to try to find Andrea Gail,
and we didn't find it.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.130
But what the film shows

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.230
is them encountering,
basically, a rogue wave.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:29.210
Rogue waves usually are not storm waves.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.860
They're a big wall of water
that's formed elsewhere

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.070
and come out of nowhere
on a otherwise calm sea.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:38.790
They used to be misbelieved.
It was a sailor's story.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:40.230
But now that we have satellite views,

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:43.680
we can actually see them from
space forming and moving.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:46.230
This wall of water is pretty big.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:48.690
Bigger than perhaps I might have thought.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.298
I think there's a little bit
more of Hollywood in that.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.330
There was nothing they could do

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:59.670
other than try to go over that wave.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.140
If you turned around and tried to run,

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.610
it would've came right down on them

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.851
and sent them straight to the bottom.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.790
In some conditions, ships do capsize.

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:13.200
When a ship capsizes,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.930
it's basically caught by
the sea and rolled over.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.390
If there's air trapped inside the hull,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.730
it can stay afloat for some time.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:22.560
In this case, what we see

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.130
is that the air gradually comes
out as the ship is moving,

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.260
and the crew deep inside are trapped.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.270
This movie captures the essence

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.600
of what it means to have
to go to work on the sea

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.790
and not be able to come home.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:39.717
For that, I give "The Perfect
Storm" a 10 out of 10.

00:11:39.717 --> 00:11:42.460
Dory: Hey, look, balloons! It is a party!

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.380
So, what we see is a wartime wreck.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:49.470
It's a submarine. As to
whose it is, hard to say.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:53.250
Looks sort of like an American-type
sub from World War II.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:55.470
In a minefield. The type
of mine with those horns?

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.132
Yeah, that's a little more common to, say,

00:11:57.132 --> 00:12:00.540
the World War I era than
the World War II era.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.460
But yes, there have been submarines

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.920
that have been found in naval minefields,

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.200
but you just wouldn't have
everything still floating around,

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.438
because after wars, they
spent a lot of time and money

00:12:09.438 --> 00:12:11.747
going around and getting
those mines out of there.

00:12:20.389 --> 00:12:21.900
That torpedo would've been deadly.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.090
Explosives can last a
long time underwater.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:25.980
Anytime we've been on
the bottom or on a wreck,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.980
you stay away, because they could go off.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:30.630
Depending on how deep the water is

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.840
and the length of the bubble
created by the explosion,

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.580
it might not be seen on the surface

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.250
and maybe only as a
slight little bubble bath,

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:40.740
as the two pelicans experience.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:42.150
My granddaughter loves it.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:45.180
And so in terms of Kaylee's satisfaction,

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:46.980
Grandpa'd give it a 10 out of 10 for that.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.140
In terms of accurately
depicting a shipwreck,

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:50.382
I'll give it a five.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:59.550
What we see here is what we
call progressive flooding.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:01.470
Ships are designed to keep water out.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:05.010
So when water starts to come
in, if you can't get it out,

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.290
if you can't keep pumping it out --

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:08.460
pumps are the only means

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:11.070
that you have to keep water
outside of the ship --

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.500
then you lose your buoyancy
and the ship will go down.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:17.130
This gives us a sense of when
you are caught in a storm

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:19.230
and your ship takes a battering

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.921
and you just can't stop
the water from coming in.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:32.970
I've been in a storm at
sea in weather like this.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.160
If they hadn't been able
to get the power back

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.960
and to get the ship upright,

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:38.670
I'd be down at the bottom of the sea now

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.020
with the rest of the team.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.550
So this brings back a
pretty powerful memory

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:48.690
of how easy it is to go
from ship to shipwreck.

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.380
I'd give this film an eight out of 10

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:55.470
in terms of depicting
a high-seas shipwreck

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:58.128
caught in the grip of
the sea and bad weather.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.587
Whale strikes did happen,
and there were angry whales

00:14:10.587 --> 00:14:13.136
that would pursue boats of whalers

00:14:13.136 --> 00:14:15.480
when they were out hunting
them to take them down.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.420
What we're looking at
is Hollywood's depiction

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.910
of Nat Philbrick's book
"In the Heart of the Sea,"

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.370
which tells the story of the whaler Essex,

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:27.150
which on the Pacific in 1819
was stove, as they said,

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:29.700
or rammed by a whale and sunk.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:32.520
But the whale damages more in this scene

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:35.910
and in a different way
than what history tells us.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.950
Owen Chase is the mate.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:41.640
His story, as well as the
account of the captain,

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:45.390
talk of a whale that may
just have been confused,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:48.180
and in that, the edges of
the planks held together

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:51.330
and keeping the water out
were pushed by the force,

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:53.400
so the water began to come in.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:56.759
But not so fast as to
immediately sink the vessel.

00:15:06.002 --> 00:15:09.150
The way they depicted things
was a little more rushed.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:12.270
It stayed afloat for enough time to elapse

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:14.520
so these guys could get supplies,

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.790
get their boats ready,
and leave without a panic.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:20.820
But what they're doing
is absolutely right.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.837
You take sails and other
things so that you can rig

00:15:23.837 --> 00:15:27.270
cover for your guys in
the sun as well as sail.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.670
And the fire, that didn't happen.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:32.978
I'll rate the film, based on
Nat's book, about a seven.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:40.860
What these guys were trying to do

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.450
is probably turn the steering a bit,

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.400
and they're turning that
starboard bow thruster

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.400
to push their vessel
off of the other side.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:50.370
So, turning bow thrusters alone,
which are down in the hull,

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:51.930
and they basically have propellers,

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:53.640
they're not really there for navigation,

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.520
and I doubt they would
have had enough effect.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:00.090
So, plausible that they knew
what was going to happen

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:02.310
and were just trying to
keep it from striking in

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:05.700
and to give them a little extra leverage

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.355
to stay off the other ship.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:17.280
Given the angle they had
it hit at, it's plausible.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.680
Those vessels are built strong.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.100
I could see that not punching
through and hanging up.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.040
Striking the other vessel like that

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.020
could have ended in them locking up,

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:29.920
certainly slowing them down a bit.

00:16:36.739 --> 00:16:38.364
[laughs] Wow.

00:16:38.364 --> 00:16:40.147
It's possible they could
have gone a little further

00:16:40.147 --> 00:16:41.970
given the speed they were,

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:44.370
but what kind of a seawall is there?

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:47.190
If it was running up on a
beach, then yeah, maybe.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:48.990
I would say that the ship

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.240
probably would've stopped before that.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.556
But then you wouldn't have had that moment

00:16:53.556 --> 00:16:56.564
where it comes in and scares the tourists

00:16:56.564 --> 00:16:58.320
and the guy with his fancy car

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:01.710
and the great scene with
the dog bark in defiance.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:06.120
I mean, that's just Hollywood
at its most dramatic best.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:09.330
I'll give this a seven out
of 10, just because it's fun

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:11.820
and it does give you a
sense of the power of ships

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:14.620
and why we try to keep them
from hitting each other.

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:23.790
It's entirely possible for a ship

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:25.620
to break apart and to split,

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:27.960
particularly in the early
days when they're coming up

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.930
with welding as opposed to
riveting ships together.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:33.690
So what we're looking at
is a depiction in film

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.840
of a real-life event where two tankers,

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.880
and the one we're looking at is Pendleton,

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.540
split in two in a storm
off New England in 1952.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.490
They're World War II-built tankers.

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:46.488
Quickly assembled, welded,

00:17:46.488 --> 00:17:48.960
and these T2 tankers, as they were called,

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.240
some of them had welding problems.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:52.800
And what happened with these ships

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:55.300
is they broke in two, bow and stern.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:06.210
It's entirely plausible.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.400
These ships, particularly
wartime-built ships,

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:09.570
were built knowing,

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:12.450
with the battle of the Atlantic
and U-boats torpedoing,

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:15.360
with watertight bulkheads,
if you lost one portion,

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:16.710
you could stay afloat.

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:18.360
In terms of navigating it,

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:20.640
purposely being steered into a shoal,

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.500
that's not what history says.

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.650
What history says is
that it drifted up there,

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:26.490
and it was driven there

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:28.350
by the force of the storm and the waves.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:30.990
So while this is a
dramatic Hollywood moment,

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:34.230
you do get the sense of
these guys keeping their head

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:38.580
and keeping steam up and some
power so that they had light.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:40.757
I'll give it a nine out of 10.

00:18:40.757 --> 00:18:44.194
At 800 feet. Passing through thermocline.

00:18:44.194 --> 00:18:46.173
All my life support is in the green.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.190
Wow. I've worn one of those suits

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.290
in a moon pool to test it out.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.150
They're called a one-atmosphere suit.

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:56.010
Basically, it's like a suit of armor

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:57.570
that you wear like a sub.

00:18:57.570 --> 00:19:00.330
Hard suits were rated

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:03.540
to go initially about
1,500 feet down to 2,000.

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:05.973
The depth they're at, perfectly right.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:09.810
Salazar: It's a phantom jellyfish.

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:11.820
The phantom jellyfish is absolutely real.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.020
I think all told,

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:16.620
less than 30 have been
spotted on deep-sea missions.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:21.620
They do glow, and those arms,
they can be about this big.

00:19:21.622 --> 00:19:24.438
They can be as deep, we've seen,

00:19:24.438 --> 00:19:27.780
as up to 21,000 feet.

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:29.430
And it's another reminder

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:33.330
that there's so much life down
there that seems alien to us

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.090
but that we are constantly encountering.

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:37.740
Salazar: I've never seen one this color.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:40.590
Salazar, we just lost Jackson's vitals.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:44.100
Checking vital signs is
absolutely part of a mission.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:45.180
You gotta do it.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:47.190
You got a human being in that.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:50.850
Not counting Namor and
vibranium and all of that,

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:53.940
in terms of depicting what these suits do,

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.730
I'd give that scene a 10 out of 10.

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:59.430
My favorite shipwreck in a movie or a show

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:01.020
has to be "Titanic,"

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.810
because it's a wreck that everybody knows,

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:05.640
and it's a wreck that has been depicted

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:07.650
in movies throughout time.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.110
And all of them ultimately focus

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:13.830
on why I, as an archeologist,
study shipwrecks.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:15.510
These are people stories,

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:19.020
and through shipwrecks we
learn more about people.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:20.490
Thank you for watching,

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:22.167
and if you enjoyed this video,

00:20:22.167 --> 00:20:24.667
why don't you click on the video above?

